      "writtenOn": "2023-03-28T15:45:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Got it. Does the alpha type of the surface matter for the second step (Dawn\u0027s intermediate -\u003e destination)? I ask because there have been other cases where we avoid conversion steps by simply lying about the source data. In other words: Just tell skia that the source is actually kPremul, which will suppress the premultiplication. You would still end up with a surface that \"thinks\" it\u0027s kPremul (even though it isn\u0027t), so if that\u0027s going to impact the upload to the texture, we\u0027d need to think more.\n\nNote that if you want to go this route, there is already an API for turning an image into a \"raw\" shader (which does no color space conversion or alpha pre-multiplication). It\u0027s intended for cases where the source data isn\u0027t necessarily RGBA, and it would require that you do a draw (rather than writePixels).\n\nActually: Color space conversion is a potential problem - if there\u0027s any chance that we need to apply a conversion to the buffer, then my first proposal (lying about the alpha type of the source) won\u0027t work - Skia will think it needs to unpremul the source before it can do the conversion, which is definitely not what you want.",
      "parentUuid": "6c5dbab5_38d420e3",
      "revId": "0c4eceec9bdf68ae94bd90319373f7e8372ddb0b",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
